# The fused lowering of `utf8` applied to `slice` skips the wrapper the oracle
# still calls, and an err records the frame it was born in — so a fusion that
# forgot to carry the wrapper's file and line made the two engines name
# different birthplaces for the same invalid byte. Nothing in the corpus read
# an invalid byte through a slice, so it went unseen. Since a range past the
# end answers none (#1890), the slice is dispatched on before it is decoded,
# and the err is still born in the wrapper.
import "std/text"

bad = [104 255 105]

fn decoded none
  ""

fn decoded piece
  text/utf8 piece

pub play = print "{decoded (text/slice bad 1 3)}"
